          <avActivityHighlights>The Avalanche Warning Service currently has only a small amount of information that has been collected in the field. &lt;br/&gt; The danger of natural avalanches will decrease gradually.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>In particular from starting zones at higher altitudes large natural avalanches, capable of reaching the valleys, must be expected. Above approximately 2300 m they can be released in the new snow and wind slab layers. &lt;br/&gt; The wind slabs will be deposited on unfavourable layers. They are to be assessed with care and prudence. &lt;br/&gt; Single backcountry tourers can release avalanches.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>Above approximately 2200 m there are 80 to 140 cm of snow, and even as much as 170 cm in some localities, especially above approximately 2600 m. &lt;br/&gt; Several medium-sized dry avalanches have been released especially on very steep slopes. &lt;br/&gt; As a consequence of the occasionally strong northwesterly wind, fresh snow drift accumulations formed on Sunday. The snowpack will become in some cases unstable. &lt;br/&gt; Towards its base, the snowpack is moist, in particular below approximately 2300 m.</snowpackStructureComment>
          <tendencyComment>The risk of spontaneous avalanches will gradually decrease.</tendencyComment>
